    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "frser-sqlite-datasource",
        "uid": "offchart"
      },
      "title": "Margin borrowing as a share of household equity holdings",
      "description": "THE PANEL TO READ. The level below is at an all-time high and this is the measure that says what that means. Read the HOUSEHOLD line: same population throughout, and it falls from 3.05% at the 2000:Q1 top and 2.80% in 2007:Q2 to 0.44% now -- a sevenfold decline, because household broker margin is roughly flat in nominal dollars since 2000 ($265bn then, $227bn now) while the equities it is measured against multiplied. The all-customers line is the same ratio on the full aggregate; the two were IDENTICAL until hedge funds entered the sector, and the gap between them after 2010 is the composition change, not a change in behaviour. A level that grows 593x since 1945 mostly records the economy getting bigger, which is why lib/metrics' third rule says a normaliser must be stated. Denominator: Z.1 FL153064105, households and nonprofit organizations' corporate equity holdings -- a stock over a stock, not a stock over an annualised flow like GDP.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 10,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 6
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "frser-sqlite-datasource",
            "uid": "offchart"
          },
          "queryType": "time series",
          "rawQueryText": "SELECT ts/1000 AS time, household_pct_of_equities AS 'households (comparable throughout)', pct_of_household_equities AS 'all customers incl. hedge funds' FROM board_leverage WHERE ts BETWEEN $__from AND $__to ORDER BY ts",
          "queryText": "SELECT ts/1000 AS time, household_pct_of_equities AS 'households (comparable throughout)', pct_of_household_equities AS 'all customers incl. hedge funds' FROM board_leverage WHERE ts BETWEEN $__from AND $__to ORDER BY ts",
          "timeColumns": [
            "time"
